"""Original Codex manuscript: Industrialisation, with fictional decision cases."""
import sys
from pathlib import Path
TD=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0,str(TD.parent))
from authoring_support import assemble

E={
 '01-00':('The process of industrialisation','Industrialisation is growth in the importance and capacity of industrial production within an economy.','It can involve investment in equipment, skills, supporting services and organised production; building one factory does not establish an economy-wide structural change.',
 'Industrial production transforms materials into goods, such as food processing or textile manufacture. Greater productive capacity can provide jobs, goods and market opportunities. The development result depends on who can participate, conditions of work and environmental effects. Capacity, actual output and the distribution of benefits are related but different measures.'),
 '02-00':('Modern production is coordinated','Modern industrial production can combine machinery, varied inputs and specialised tasks that need coordination.','Supporting infrastructure enables production, but naming land, labour and capital is not the same as describing the networks and services connecting activities.',
 'Infrastructure includes transport, electricity, water and communication networks. The factors-of-production classification concerns inputs; infrastructure concerns supporting systems. In that input classification, money capital finances resources and capital goods are produced assets used in production, as Topic 3.1 explains. Categories can overlap: a road is a produced asset, yet is discussed as transport infrastructure when its network role matters. Test what the question asks rather than treating the classifications as mutually exclusive lists.'),
 '02-01':('Machinery and advanced technology','Complex machinery and advanced technology can provide control, precision or productive capacity beyond simpler methods.','Their productive value depends on suitable tasks, skilled operation, reliable inputs and maintenance.',
 'A computer-controlled cutter in an invented garment factory can repeat specified shapes accurately. If no technician can repair a fault, that potential capacity may stand idle. Identify the feature from its productive role, not just the fact that a device looks modern.'),
 '02-02':('Several raw materials','An industrial product can require several raw materials or components whose quantities and quality must fit the process.','A shortage of one essential input can interrupt completion even when the other materials are available.',
 'A fictional garment producer combines fabric, thread, fasteners and labels. Buying more fabric will not complete an order that lacks suitable fasteners. A wide range of materials means coordination of supply and specifications, not an assertion that every industry requires the same number of inputs.'),
 '02-03':('Specialised industrial tasks','Complex division of labour assigns different specialised tasks or stages to workers and teams.','Specialist skills can improve task performance while creating dependence between stages.',
 'Designers, cutters, machine operators, quality checkers and dispatch workers perform different roles in a garment process. Naming the total workforce does not show the division. Explain what each relevant stage contributes and how an error in one stage affects the next.'),
 '02-04':('Coordinate the stages','Coordination of specialised tasks arranges the timing, flow and information linking production stages.','An unbalanced flow can create queues or idle capacity even when each stage has capable workers.',
 'If cutting produces 100 sets a day while stitching completes 60, unfinished sets accumulate unless the imbalance is addressed. Changing schedules, addressing the slow stage or matching output between stages is more useful than simply demanding that cutters work faster.'),
 '03-00':('Strategies answer different questions','Import substitution develops domestic production to replace imports, while export-orientated production is directed primarily towards foreign markets.','Strategy, scale and ownership are different dimensions; a locally owned factory can be export-orientated or replace imports.',
 'Exports are sales abroad; export-orientated industrialisation is a strategy for building productive capacity around foreign demand. One export sale does not demonstrate that strategy. Compare alternatives against a stated aim using demand, resources, feasibility and who benefits. An ownership label is not evidence of efficiency.'),
 '03-01':('Import substitution','Import substitution means developing domestic production to replace goods previously imported.','It may build local productive capacity and reduce dependence on some imports, but needs viable demand, suitable inputs and the ability to produce acceptable goods.',
 'In a fictional case, local producers can meet a steady demand for school bags with available skills and material supply. Replacing imported bags may support work and learning. If the domestic process is very costly or still depends on imported inputs, the benefit is narrower than a claim of complete self-sufficiency. Protecting production without improvement may leave users paying more for poor quality.'),
 '03-02':('Export-orientated industry','Export-orientated production means producing goods or services primarily for sale in foreign markets.','Access to a larger market can support expansion, but producers must meet delivery, quality and cost requirements and face changes in overseas demand.',
 'Confirmed foreign orders can make a larger output worthwhile when transport and inputs are reliable. A producer without a feasible delivery route may fail those orders. Foreign sales can bring earnings, yet dependence on one buyer or product can make local work vulnerable to external changes. Judge the supply and demand conditions rather than assuming a larger market guarantees success.'),
 '03-03':('Large and small scale','Large-scale production can spread some fixed costs and use specialised equipment, while small-scale production may fit limited markets and flexible local tasks.','The suitable scale depends on demand, finance, coordination and local resources; greater size alone does not establish lower average cost or wider benefits.',
 'A large standardised order may justify equipment that would be underused by a small repair business. A small workshop may adapt products more easily but lack finance for expensive machinery. Compare the same output need, include spare capacity and ask whether a claimed economy of scale actually lowers cost per useful unit.'),
 '03-04':('Multinational investment','Multinational companies operate across countries and may bring finance, technology or links to wider markets.','Local gains depend on jobs, training, purchasing, taxation and environmental practice; profits and important decisions may also leave the host economy.',
 'An invented investor offering equipment and training can help remove a capacity constraint. If local suppliers are excluded and no skills are transferred, the local link is weaker. Evaluate enforceable arrangements and actual retained benefits. Do not promise that a company will train, hire or buy locally merely because these outcomes are possible.'),
 '03-05':('Local investors and joint enterprises','Local investors organise finance and decisions from within the economy; a joint enterprise combines participation from partners, which may include local and foreign investors.','Partners can combine local knowledge with finance or technology, but need workable agreements about decisions, contributions and benefits.',
 'Local ownership can support retained earnings and responsiveness to users, but does not remove finance or skill shortages. A joint venture might bridge those gaps, while exposing disagreement over control. Compare a feasible partnership with what the local producer could achieve alone; nationality is not a substitute for evidence about performance.'),
 '03-06':('State enterprises','State enterprises organise production under state ownership and can pursue strategic services or access goals.','They need finance, capable management, maintenance and accountability; public ownership alone does not establish efficient or equitable delivery.',
 'A fictional state provider may extend a service to sparsely served communities where commercial returns are limited. It must still sustain the operation and use resources well. Compare reliable access, cost and implementation with a feasible alternative, rather than treating either state or private ownership as an automatic answer.'),
 '03-07':('Public private partnership','A public private partnership is an arrangement in which public and private participants share specified responsibilities for providing infrastructure or a service.','It can combine capabilities and resources, but its costs, risks, access requirements and accountability depend on the agreement and its implementation.',
 'Public ownership may be retained while a private partner finances or operates a specified facility. That differs from a simple transfer of ownership. A contract that leaves costly failures with the public while excluding low-income users may serve the development aim poorly. Assess responsibilities and realistic enforcement rather than the partnership label.'),
 '04-00':('NIC examples and the state','The syllabus names South Africa, India, the Philippines and Indonesia as illustrative newly industrialising countries; these are syllabus-context examples rather than a current official classification list.','State provision of infrastructure, skills or enabling arrangements can support industrial capacity, but the effects depend on relevance, implementation and opportunity cost.',
 'A state can help address a missing road connection or skill shortage. It can also spend on facilities that remain unused. To evaluate its role, trace the constraint, the action and the evidence of productive or social benefit. The fictional examples here model that reasoning and are not claims that a named country used a particular policy or achieved a stated result.'),
 '05-00':('Industries and development in Namibia','Mining, fishing, agriculture, textiles and tourism illustrate different routes through which production can contribute to Namibia’s economic and social development.','Employment, local purchases, processing and public revenue are potential benefits; resource depletion, pollution, insecure work or benefits leaving the area can limit gains.',
 'Mining can supply raw materials and earnings, fishing can link catches with processing, agriculture can support food and livelihoods, textiles can create manufactured goods, and tourism can create service demand. These are mechanisms, not current contribution statistics. Tourism increases local income when local workers, owners or suppliers participate and retain earnings. A site attracting visitors alone does not show that local households benefit.'),
 '06-00':('Sustainable production improvements','Possible production improvements include value-added processes, investment partnerships, suitable modern processes and research into resources or methods.','A sustainable improvement addresses a real productive constraint while considering resources, costs, capabilities, access and environmental effects.',
 'If suitable raw material is wasted because storage fails, better handling may increase useful output more directly than expanding extraction. If an order requires a new quality standard, a controlled process and skills may be needed. Select the improvement from the evidence instead of prescribing every strategy in every setting.'),
 '06-01':('Value-added processes','Value-added processes transform or improve an input into a product with additional economic value.','Local processing can retain additional activity and skills where demand, viable costs and suitable capacity exist; a higher selling price alone does not establish a higher net gain.',
 'An invented group may process suitable fruit into a stable product rather than lose it before sale. Processing needs equipment, quality control, packaging and buyers. Compare the additional value with the additional costs and losses avoided; do not count the whole sale price as the extra value created.'),
 '06-02':('Foreign and local joint ventures','A joint venture combines partners’ contributions to a specified enterprise or activity, potentially linking foreign finance or technology with local knowledge and participation.','Benefits require compatible aims and workable terms for control, skills and retained returns.',
 'An invented local processor with buyers but insufficient equipment finance might gain capacity through a partner. Without clear contributions, training and decision rules, the arrangement can create dependence or conflict. A partnership should address the documented gap and protect feasible local participation rather than merely add a foreign name.'),
 '06-03':('Modern production processes','Modern processes can improve control, efficiency or quality where they suit the task and supporting resources.','Reducing defects or avoidable losses can increase useful output without a proportional rise in raw-material extraction.',
 'A fictional food processor with temperature-related spoilage might improve monitoring and control. The system needs staff able to use and maintain it. A sophisticated device that is not used correctly may add expense without reducing losses. Improvement is shown by comparable usable output and quality, not simply by equipment purchased.'),
 '06-04':('Research and exploration','Research gathers and tests information about products, processes or conditions; exploration investigates the location and characteristics of resources.','Better evidence can reduce uncertainty and guide feasible decisions, but does not guarantee a commercially or environmentally viable discovery.',
 'Testing whether a local material meets a quality requirement can prevent an unsuitable investment. Discovering a deposit does not establish access, recoverability, an affordable productive method or acceptable impacts. Describe what uncertainty the enquiry addresses and how the result would change the decision.')
}

X={
 '01':('An expansion with conditions','In an invented district, processing equipment and training allow more local raw material to be made into finished goods. Capacity and industrial activity increase, which illustrates industrialisation. Whether households benefit also depends on wages, access to work and resource effects. If untreated waste damages water, the output gain coexists with a development cost. Topic 3.1 explains the inputs; Topic 1.1 supplies the wider development dimensions. Use the process definition and a mechanism rather than calling any new shop an industrial transformation.'),
 '02':('An original production comparison','In a fictional factory, cutting capacity is 100 sets daily and stitching completes 60. Completed output is 60 and the unfinished queue grows by 40 sets a day. After a suitable stitching improvement, cutting remains 100 and stitching completes 90, so the queue grows by 10. The smaller imbalance accompanies higher finished output. Faster cutting alone would increase the backlog. Topic 3.1 explains specialised tasks; this example links their flow and coordination to useful industrial output.'),
 '03':('Choose a strategy against the same aim','Fictional District A wants reliable local employment. A modest domestic market has steady demand and producers can match imported product quality with available skills. A small import-substitution operation is plausible. A much larger export plant would require finance and a reliable shipping route that the district lacks. Under these facts, the smaller operation is better supported, although its potential market is limited. If confirmed foreign demand and a workable route become available, the comparison changes. Strategies can be combined over time: an enterprise may first learn to serve local users and later supply foreign buyers. That is a possible route, not a promise that each stage will occur. For ownership choices, compare who supplies finance, who decides, who bears risk and who retains benefits. The same questions distinguish private investment, state activity and partnership arrangements. Topic 3.1 supplies enterprise and economies of scale; Topic 9.1 develops trade.'),
 '04':('Evaluate an illustrative state action','In a fictional NIC-style case, firms have confirmed buyers and trained staff, but a damaged shared road prevents delivery. A state-funded repair with maintenance could address a constraint that individual firms cannot easily solve. If deliveries then become possible, infrastructure has supported capacity use. A second proposal offers unused factory buildings while leaving the road blocked; it is less directly relevant under these facts. This illustrates how to evaluate state action against the productive constraint. It is not an account of events in South Africa, India, the Philippines or Indonesia. Country histories and current rankings are outside this lesson. Topic 2.2 links public plans to implementation and outcomes. A named country or announced expenditure is insufficient evidence of success.'),
 '05':('Trace who receives the gain','In a fictional coastal processing activity, a group buys local catches and hires local workers for additional processing, with suitable demand and maintained resource limits. Local wages and supplier earnings can rise because households participate in the added activity. If the same raw fish were sent away with no local processing, those particular processing gains would occur elsewhere. Yet more extraction is not automatically sustainable: catch management and the condition of the stock still matter. This is a conceptual illustration, not a claim about any real Namibian company or present fishery. Topic 1.2 explains resource sustainability; Topic 5.1 explores rural livelihoods. Connect an industry to employment, income, skills or services and also identify the condition that makes the benefit plausible.'),
 '06':('Increase usable output before extracting more','In an invented processor, 100 units of input yield 70 saleable units and 30 spoiled units. After a suitable handling change, the same input yields 90 saleable and 10 spoiled units. Useful output rises by 20 while spoilage falls by 20; more extraction is not required for this gain. The change needs working equipment, appropriate staff skills and buyers for the usable product. An unexplained before-and-after pattern alone cannot isolate the cause, so a real evaluation would check whether relevant conditions changed. Topic 3.1 explains production and appropriate technology; Topic 1.2 asks whether improvements can last. The evidence here supports investigating avoidable losses rather than immediately seeking a larger raw-material supply.')
}
